<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gough Whitlam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/2014">2014</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gough Whitlam was the Labor prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> from 1972 to 1975. His government ended conscription and withdrew from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, formally ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-australia-policy">White Australia policy</a> and introduced universal health insurance and free university education. On 11 November 1975 he was dismissed by Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sir-john-kerr">Sir John Kerr</a> after the Senate blocked supply.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gough-whitlam.jpg" alt="Gough Whitlam" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Work of the Australian Government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gough_Whitlam_at_the_Lodge_1974.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The constitutional crisis of 1975, in which Governor-General Sir John Kerr dismissed the Labor government of <b>Gough Whitlam</b> after the Senate blocked supply, remains controversial.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Supporters of the Australian Governor-General’s dismissal of the prime minister <b>Gough Whitlam</b> in 1975, after the Senate had blocked the budget, saw it as proof that reserve powers could break a deadlock and check politicians who exceeded their powers.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Australian constitutional crisis of 1975 the governor-general, Sir John Kerr, dismissed Prime Minister <b>Gough Whitlam</b>, who still held a majority in the House of Representatives, after the Senate had blocked the budget, a use of reserve powers that is still disputed.</blockquote><a href="/government/parliamentary-democracy">Parliamentary democracy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1973, when Prime Minister <b>Gough Whitlam</b> visited the People's Republic of China, it was played through loudspeakers along his motorcade's route from Peking International Airport to his hotel.</blockquote><a href="/songs/click-go-the-shears">Click Go the Shears</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gough Whitlam's birthplace, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney">Sydney</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-37.81,145.05&amp;year=1979" aria-label="Gough Whitlam's birthplace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8169 3523 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gough Whitlam"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8619 3793h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8619 3793h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-37.81,145.05&amp;year=1979">What was here in 1979? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
